<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nakba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as The Catastrophe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nakba, Arabic for catastrophe, is the Palestinian name for the flight and expulsion of roughly 700,000 Palestinian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> from their homes during the war surrounding Israel's creation in 1948, and for the destruction of hundreds of their villages. It made refugees of more than half the Arab population of former Mandatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, scattered them across the region, and stands at the centre of Palestinian memory and of the still unresolved refugee question.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nakba.jpg" alt="Nakba" width="330" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benno Rothenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tantura_expulsion_(997008136022405171)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The word nakba means misfortune or calamity in Arabic. Its first use for the defeat of 1948 is attributed to the Syrian historian Constantin Zureiq, a professor at the American University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, in his 1948 book Ma'na an-Nakba (The Meaning of the Disaster). Before 1948 the Arab phrase for a year of catastrophe referred to 1920, when European powers partitioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><p>The UN Partition Plan of 1947 assigned 56 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> to a Jewish state and 44 per cent to the Arab state, although Palestinians were the majority of the population. Small local clashes began on 30 November 1947 and escalated until March 1948. Some Palestinians had already begun to leave, expecting to return after the fighting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Between December 1947 and March 1948, between 70,000 and 100,000 Palestinians, mostly from the urban middle and upper classes, were expelled or fled. Historians record massacres at Al-Khisas on 18 December 1947 and at Balad al-Shaykh on 31 December. Arab League armies entered Mandatory Palestine on 15 May 1948, after the Mandate ended, and expulsions and the destruction of villages then spread through rural areas. The Tantura massacre took place on 22 and 23 May.</p><p>Fighting and expulsions continued into the autumn, with the depopulation of Beersheba on 21 October and massacres at al-Dawayima and Safsaf on 29 October. On 11 December 1948 the UN passed Resolution 194, which declared that refugees wishing to return should be permitted to do so and that compensation should be paid for lost property. Armistices were signed between February and July 1949. By the end of the war most of the Palestinians living in the territory that became Israel had been expelled or had fled, and over 500 villages and eleven towns were destroyed or depopulated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Israel prevented refugees outside its borders from returning, and Palestinians who remained were placed under martial law until 1966. A nationality law of 14 July 1952 ended their former Palestinian citizenship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> names replaced many Arabic place names. A second displacement followed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967, known as the Naksa, or setback. Today most Palestinians live outside the area of Mandatory Palestine.</p><p>The Palestinian national narrative treats the Nakba as a formative collective trauma, and Palestinians mark Nakba Day on 15 May. The Israeli national narrative places the same events within the War of Independence that established the state, and Israeli officials have often held that many Palestinians left willingly. Since the 1970s Israeli historians known as the New Historians have challenged that account. Some Israeli scholars in turn argue that Palestinian memory leaves out the attacks made by Arab irregular forces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nakba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scholars such as Joseph Massad, Edward Said and Nur Masalha describe Zionism as colonial or settler colonial, and Palestinians such as Noura Erakat and Mohammed El-Kurd see it as inseparable from the <b>Nakba</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the United Nations voted to partition Palestine in 1947, David Ben-Gurion declared independence on 14 May 1948; the war that followed secured the new state against the armies of its neighbours, while some 700,000 Palestinian Arabs fled or were driven from their homes, remembered as the <b>Nakba</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/israel">Israel</a></li><li><blockquote>The Palestine Liberation Organization, founded in 1964 and led from 1969 by Yasser Arafat, came to stand for Palestinian nationhood after the <b>Nakba</b> of 1948 had scattered much of the Arab population of Mandatory Palestine.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-palestine">State of Palestine</a></li></ul></section>
